hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the  central ideas which runs throughout all  of Plato's works in which Socrates is  one of the main characters but  particularly exemplified here in the  credo is this notion of living in  accordance with what we would call  principles some sort of doctrine some  sort of fixed point that one holds to  until one find something something  better something more adequate than that  and this is really integral to the  entire Socratic and and platonic  approach this is something that's  distinctive about Socrates part of the  reason why he's actually going to get  executed notice to put living or dying  in accordance with principle because in  this case the question is whether he  should flee Athens and violate the the  things that he is held dear the things  that he's taught the things that he has  put forth to the public the things that  he and his friends have agreed on or  whether he should stick with those  regardless of what the consequences are  going to be in this case the consequence  being is death by execution and a rather  unjust way so what we mean by my  in accordance with principle it means  sticking with what you've actually  settled on saying that I'm not going to  change things from case to case to case  just to suit my whims just to advance my  interest just to avoid some pain just to  get some pleasure nothing is as  important as consistency you might say  and Socrates's is saying this in  response to credo because credo has  already advanced these arguments which  I've talked about in another core  concept video look socrates I've got the  guard bribed I've got a ship ready I've  got a place for you to go you know  money's no object I'm rich I'm happy to  do this let's get the hell out of here  and Socrates says let's think this  through and here's one of the responses  that he's making to credo in this but  this is also something that you're gonna  see in other dialogues as well he says  didn't we say that this is the case  didn't we say that this is the case  don't we have to follow through on those  things  what are we just gonna say well you know  normally I go along with this rule but  if it's a it's a matter of dying then  then I can bend the rules a bit for  myself Socrates is saying no you can't  bend that rule it's not a rule if you  can bend it it's not a principle if  you're going to set it aside whenever  it's convenient for you so this is  really an important idea here now that  doesn't mean that he's totally  inflexible he also is willing to reflect  he says credo let's think about this is  this a case in which we could abrogate  this principle are there any better  principles than we could appeal to you  you can be open to persuasion he says  credo go ahead and make your case as a  matter of fact I'll try to make the case  to myself I'll think this through and  see if I can't decide that it would in  fact be all right for me to flee Athens  so he's not  he's not just being stubborn he's not  saying look once I've decided on  something I'm never going to change it  think of this as sort of like mountain  climbing when you're mountain climbing  and you've got some fixed points that  you've you know I forget that the exact  equipment somebody I'm sure will put it  in a comment you know you hammer the the  peg into the mountain you're not going  to leave that peg behind until you've  got a better pack so Socrates is saying  this works this way with ideas it works  this way with our frame of reference it  works this way with our life it works  this way with the consistency of their  behavior and our relationships with  other people including those with whom  were in society or you know a political  community so what are in fact the  principles that he brings up here in  other dialogues he's going to say to his  interlocutors hey didn't we already  agree that there's something like you  know a doctrine of recollection that  would be another example of a principle  that he doesn't feel they have to prove  every single time and he's not going to  abandon until he comes up with something  better in this case there's actually  three ones that are enunciated or  articulated explicitly and then there's  another one that kind of slips under the  radar and we'll come to that one last  because it brings it all together so he  spends a lot of time and I'm actually  shooting another core concepts  specifically on this on this particular  you might say epistemological principle  we shouldn't accept the opinions of just  anybody and we shouldn't care about what  the many think because we should care  about the opinions of those who are  actually wise or knowledgeable or expert  or authorities or competent all these  different names get used and basically  the idea behind this is we should only  pay attention to what the people who  actually have some sort of justification  for their beliefs  actually have to say to us so in  horsemanship we don't pay attention to  the crowd we pay attention to the person  who actually has some competency in  animal husbandry in finance we go to the  accountant or we go to the financial  adviser in medicine we don't ask you  know uncle ray or you know the guy on  the street we ask a doctor and and we  could go down the line for each of these  so when it comes to matters of right and  wrong we don't worry about what the  crowd thinks we worried Socrates says  about that one guy who actually has his  head on straight with respect to these  you know moral principles or you know  ethics in general as a system that guy  we pay attention to him turns out  Socrates will you know be the closest  thing that we can come to it in this  this dialogue so that's one really  important principle another principle  that Socrates uses here which we see  coming up in other Socratic dialogues as  well is that one should never willingly  do wrong to another person and this is a  very broad extent what does he mean by  do wrong there one should never  willingly harm unjustly another person  so that also means one shouldn't  retaliate returning evil for evil  according to Socrates it's very much  like the Sermon on the Mount idea of  turning the other cheek and you see this  in the gorgeous you see this in several  other platonic dialogues this was a  consistent doctrine of Socrates you can  say well what about going to war stuff  like that he actually talks about you  know if you're ordered to go out to war  by the state then you you are justified  in doing that but as a private citizen  one should never willingly harm other  people whether it's one's fellow  citizens one's children resident aliens  from another community you know in this  case  also the laws he's going to talk about  and the third one that's particularly  interesting  that's a negatively framed moral  principle here's a positively framed  moral principle when you made an  agreement with somebody you should  follow through on that agreement it's  the right thing to do it's wrong not to  follow through on that so for each of  these Socrates is responding to creative  and saying look we don't care what the  many think we're interested in what the  the competent person would say the  dialectician the one who actually thinks  things out the moral philosopher we're  interested in what they think and you  know people are going to say that you  should you should you know flee Socrates  that's going to violate these two other  principles because in doing so I'm  actually going to be retaliating against  evil that's been done to me by doing  evil in turn that's a wrong thing to do  even if it caused me to lose my life you  know dying in accordance with principle  I shouldn't do it and I made an  agreement when I walked into that  courtroom and I said okay I'm gonna do a  jury trial I was making an agreement  that I was going to accept the verdict  of the jury and go along with it I  wasn't I wasn't saying I get to take  back you know whatever whatever happens  I get to you know set aside the the  verdict because then I would be placing  myself above the entire legal apparatus  and I would be saying that I get to make  exceptions for myself because I feel  scared or I don't want to die or it  would be inconvenient for me to die at  this time or anything along those lines  now notice that there is a sort of meta  principle governing all of this which he  says a little bit earlier on in his  discussion with credo he says that we  should follow principles until we find  some better ones and if we can't find  some better ones  credo these are the three that we have  in this case these are the three that  are operative you might say so unless  you can put some other stuff on the  table in discussion and of course credo  can't although we might be able to as  interlocutors of Socrates I'm going to  have to go along with the verdict and  stay here and thanks for you know making  all these arrangements credo but I can't  follow them because it's more important  to me to actually follow through on the  kind of life that I've been living this  entire time a life in which I was  devoted to trying to find the right way  and to stick with it and not inflexibly  of course but you know something that I  could rely on something that other  people could look at and say yeah that  guy you can trust him there could be  some better principles but unless you  actually demonstrate them if you already  have some principles Socrates is saying  you got to follow those so that is like  I said a meta principle it's a principle  about how you want to follow these  principles in this case but it's also  one about you know principle in general  so like I said at the beginning this is  something that is really central to  Socrates entire approach to morality and  Plato's approach to morality and you're  going to see this in other thinkers  throughout history as well in part  because you know what he's talking about  here is something that goes beyond Plato  or Socrates and beyond ancient Athens or  even ancient Greece it's talking about  consistency
 

